{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJW87nXQ9TvqDAkDHFwP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RzSZMqhJ5GbFCWdfz5jHLr","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; python3 - <<'E'\nt=open('answer.md').read()\ni=t.index('- If s>K:')\nj=t.index('So K odd gives')\nnew='''- If s>K: z_1=K−v−w<u=p_1, so z dominates p only via pair (2,3): t<v and K−u−t<w, i.e. t∈[K−u−w+1, v−1]. Every t∈[0,K−u] other than v and y_2:=K−u−w must lie in this range. The endpoint t=0 is not in the range (y_2≥0), and t=K−u is not in it either (it would need K−u≤v−1, but x_3=K−u−v≥0). So both 0 and K−u lie in {v,y_2}. If K=u then v=w=0, s=K, excluded; so 0≠K−u and {0,K−u}={v,y_2}. Either v=0,y_2=K−u: then x_3=K−u, w=0, s=u≤K, contradicting s>K; or v=K−u, y_2=0: i.e. K−u−v=0 and K−u−w=0.\n Applying the same argument to the lines p_2=v (opposite y) and p_3=w (opposite x), by symmetry also K−v−u=0, K−v−w=0. So u+v=u+w=v+w=K, hence u=v=w=K/2, K even, and V={(K/2,K/2,0),(K/2,0,K/2),(0,K/2,K/2)}.\n\n'''\nt=t[:i]+new+t[j:]\nt=t[:t.index('Note on rigor')].rstrip()+'\\n'\nopen('answer.md','w').write(t)\nE\ntail -8 answer.md; echo \"[sonnet2] cleaned s>K subcase in answer.md; FINAL\" >> board.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2913,"cache_read_input_tokens":27094,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2913,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17259f19-7177-479b-b61d-27885badb56c","uuid":"81b8ade3-a149-4a2d-9ee3-7f0ed5c6dafc","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:58.203Z","request_id":"req_011CgJW87Tg7VYSWkh5Q6Hz3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RzSZMqhJ5GbFCWdfz5jHLr","type":"tool_result","content":"The configuration is symmetric under permuting the players (and (u,v,w) accordingly): each of x,y,z is the \"opposite\" of one shared value.\n\n*Line test.* Consider the line P={p: p_1=u}, p=(u,t,K−u−t), 0≤t≤K−u. No p∈P is dominated by x or y (same first coordinate and the other two coordinates have the same sum as x's resp. y's, so p can't be smaller in both). So every p∈P∖{x,y} (t≠v, t≠K−u−w) must be dominated by z=(K−v−w,v,w).\n- If s<K: the t strictly between v and K−u−w (an interval, nonempty-width K−s). For such t, p_2=t>v and p_3=K−u−t>w, so p_2≥z_2, p_3≥z_3 and z can't dominate p. Hence there is no t strictly between: K−s=1, i.e. s=K−1. Then x=(u,v,w+1), y=(u,v+1,w), z=(u+1,v,w), and since K≥2 some of u,v,w is ≥1, say w≥1 (others symmetric). Then p=(u+1,v+1,w−1)∉V is dominated by none of x,y,z (against each it is smaller in only one coordinate). Contradiction.\n- If s>K: z_1=K−v−w<u=p_1, so z dominates p only via pair (2,3): t<v and K−u−t<w, i.e. t∈[K−u−w+1, v−1]. Every t∈[0,K−u] other than v and y_2:=K−u−w must lie in this range. The endpoint t=0 is not in the range (y_2≥0), and t=K−u is not in it either (it would need K−u≤v−1, but x_3=K−u−v≥0). So both 0 and K−u lie in {v,y_2}. If K=u then v=w=0, s=K, excluded; so 0≠K−u and {0,K−u}={v,y_2}. Either v=0,y_2=K−u: then x_3=K−u, w=0, s=u≤K, contradicting s>K; or v=K−u, y_2=0: i.e. K−u−v=0 and K−u−w=0.\n Applying the same argument to the lines p_2=v (opposite y) and p_3=w (opposite x), by symmetry also K−v−u=0, K−v−w=0. So u+v=u+w=v+w=K, hence u=v=w=K/2, K even, and V={(K/2,K/2,0),(K/2,0,K/2),(0,K/2,K/2)}.\n\nSo K odd gives no stable set, and K even gives exactly the symmetric one. ∎","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17259f19-7177-479b-b61d-27885badb56c","uuid":"75d7c643-61a9-4e36-9448-d56156ae7996","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:58.242Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"The configuration is symmetric under permuting the players (and (u,v,w) accordingly): each of x,y,z is the \"opposite\" of one shared value.\n\n*Line test.* Consider the line P={p: p_1=u}, p=(u,t,K−u−t), 0≤t≤K−u. No p∈P is dominated by x or y (same first coordinate and the other two coordinates have the same sum as x's resp. y's, so p can't be smaller in both). So every p∈P∖{x,y} (t≠v, t≠K−u−w) must be dominated by z=(K−v−w,v,w).\n- If s<K: the t strictly between v and K−u−w (an interval, nonempty-width K−s). For such t, p_2=t>v and p_3=K−u−t>w, so p_2≥z_2, p_3≥z_3 and z can't dominate p. Hence there is no t strictly between: K−s=1, i.e. s=K−1. Then x=(u,v,w+1), y=(u,v+1,w), z=(u+1,v,w), and since K≥2 some of u,v,w is ≥1, say w≥1 (others symmetric). Then p=(u+1,v+1,w−1)∉V is dominated by none of x,y,z (against each it is smaller in only one coordinate). Contradiction.\n- If s>K: z_1=K−v−w<u=p_1, so z dominates p only via pair (2,3): t<v and K−u−t<w, i.e. t∈[K−u−w+1, v−1]. Every t∈[0,K−u] other than v and y_2:=K−u−w must lie in this range. The endpoint t=0 is not in the range (y_2≥0), and t=K−u is not in it either (it would need K−u≤v−1, but x_3=K−u−v≥0). So both 0 and K−u lie in {v,y_2}. If K=u then v=w=0, s=K, excluded; so 0≠K−u and {0,K−u}={v,y_2}. Either v=0,y_2=K−u: then x_3=K−u, w=0, s=u≤K, contradicting s>K; or v=K−u, y_2=0: i.e. K−u−v=0 and K−u−w=0.\n Applying the same argument to the lines p_2=v (opposite y) and p_3=w (opposite x), by symmetry also K−v−u=0, K−v−w=0. So u+v=u+w=v+w=K, hence u=v=w=K/2, K even, and V={(K/2,K/2,0),(K/2,0,K/2),(0,K/2,K/2)}.\n\nSo K odd gives no stable set, and K even gives exactly the symmetric one. ∎","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RzSZMqhJ5GbFCWdfz5jHLr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJW8V3CZRWXvnUbWQAC6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`/work/answer.md` now holds the answer and a full proof. I have not written `/work/SUBMIT`, so the attempt is still open. The board was empty when I started, and `sonnet` posted nothing during my run, so the answer reflects only my own work.\n\n- **Question 1:** a stable set exists exactly when K is even.\n- **Question 2:** for K = 2m the stable set is unique: {(0,m,m), (m,0,m), (m,m,0)}. For odd K there is none.\n- **Check:** an exhaustive search for K = 2 to 11 (`/work/enum.py`) found exactly one stable set for each even K (2, 4, …, 10) and none for each odd K (3, 5, …, 11). The even-K sets are the symmetric ones above.\n\nThe proof rests on one observation: two allocations avoid dominating each other exactly when they agree in some coordinate. A stable set is therefore a family in which every pair agrees somewhere. There are two cases:\n\n- **A single coordinate is constant across the set:** the set would have to be an entire line of allocations. The allocation with the next-higher value in that coordinate is then undominated, so this case is impossible.\n- **No coordinate is constant:** the set is forced to be a triangle of exactly three allocations with pairwise shared values u, v, w. Testing the line through the shared value u shows that u = v = w = K/2.\n\nSo K must be even and the set is the symmetric one. The same argument gives existence for even K. I appended a FINAL entry to `/work/board.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1623,"cache_read_input_tokens":30007,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1623,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":503,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17259f19-7177-479b-b61d-27885badb56c","uuid":"c4355942-0441-4004-a6c2-3fbd820042f0","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:02:03.085Z","request_id":"req_011CgJW8UfsHHz6LCVRmFE3h"}
