n-K sets are the symmetric ones above.\n\nThe proof rests on one observation: two allocations avoid dominating each other exactly when they agree in some coordinate. A stable set is therefore a family in which every pair agrees somewhere. There are two cases:\n\n- **A single coordinate is constant across the set:** the set would have to be an entire line of allocations. The allocation with the next-higher value in that coordinate is then undominated, so this case is impossible.\n- **No coordinate is constant:** the set is forced to be a triangle of exactly three allocations with pairwise shared values u, v, w. Testing the line through the shared value u shows that u = v = w = K/2.\n\nSo K must be even and the set is the symmetric one. The same argument gives existence for even K. I appended a FINAL entry to `/work/board.md`.","ttft_ms":1761,"type":"result","duration_ms":106911,"uuid":"e821a22e-016c-4560-b97d-edf096b7b69d","ttft_stream_ms":1495,"time_to_request_ms":78,"first_content_frame_ms":1495,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":14,"outputTokens":13083,"cacheReadInputTokens":138959,"cacheCreationInputTokens":20427,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1958214,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":8088,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}}}
