{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018jvFsQdCzEKX4Uktj4ExVV","type":"tool_result","content":"**Step 3b: e = −g < 0.** Then v_k ≥ g for all k, because P_k ≥ 0. Let u = v+f with Σf = −g, and let N = {i : f_i < 0}.\n- P_k dominates u iff two of these hold: f_i < 0 for i≠k, or f_k < −g.\n- If |N| ≥ 2, some P_k dominates u: take k to be a third index, or any k if |N| = 3.\n- If |N| = 1, N = {i}, then no P_k dominates u. Indeed for k≠i the only available strict coordinate is i, and for k = i, f_k < −g would need the other entries to be negative.\n- |N| = 0 is impossible, since Σf < 0.\n\nSo every u with exactly one negative f_i must lie in V, and V's elements are f = −g·e_k. Suppose some v_i ≥ g+1. Take f_i = −g−1, f_j = 1 and f_k = 0 for the other two indices. Then u ≥ 0 has |N| = 1 and u ∉ V. This contradicts external stability. So v_i = g for all i. Then K = 3g − g\n\nHence odd K has no stable set, and for even K = 2m any stable set equals the symmetric set S_m.\n\n**Step 4: S_m is stable for K = 2m.**\n- Internal: any two of its elements agree in a coordinate (value m or 0), so by Lemma 1 neither dominates the other.\n- External: (0,m,m) dominates y iff y_2 < m and y_3 < m, and similarly for the other two elements. So y is undominated by S_m iff, for each i, some coordinate other than i is ≥ m. In particular, then at least two coordinates are ≥ m. With sum 2m, y is a permutation of (m,m,0), which lies in S_m.\n\n∎","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17259f19-7177-479b-b61d-27885badb56c","uuid":"f4f741db-c37d-477a-80b9-80e875b86aad","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:02:09.452Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"**Step 3b: e = −g < 0.** Then v_k ≥ g for all k, because P_k ≥ 0. Let u = v+f with Σf = −g, and let N = {i : f_i < 0}.\n- P_k dominates u iff two of these hold: f_i < 0 for i≠k, or f_k < −g.\n- If |N| ≥ 2, some P_k dominates u: take k to be a third index, or any k if |N| = 3.\n- If |N| = 1, N = {i}, then no P_k dominates u. Indeed for k≠i the only available strict coordinate is i, and for k = i, f_k < −g would need the other entries to be negative.\n- |N| = 0 is impossible, since Σf < 0.\n\nSo every u with exactly one negative f_i must lie in V, and V's elements are f = −g·e_k. Suppose some v_i ≥ g+1. Take f_i = −g−1, f_j = 1 and f_k = 0 for the other two indices. Then u ≥ 0 has |N| = 1 and u ∉ V. This contradicts external stability. So v_i = g for all i. Then K = 3g − g\n\nHence odd K has no stable set, and for even K = 2m any stable set equals the symmetric set S_m.\n\n**Step 4: S_m is stable for K = 2m.**\n- Internal: any two of its elements agree in a coordinate (value m or 0), so by Lemma 1 neither dominates the other.\n- External: (0,m,m) dominates y iff y_2 < m and y_3 < m, and similarly for the other two elements. So y is undominated by S_m iff, for each i, some coordinate other than i is ≥ m. In particular, then at least two coordinates are ≥ m. With sum 2m, y is a permutation of (m,m,0), which lies in S_m.\n\n∎","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018jvFsQdCzEKX4Uktj4ExVV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
